---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../site';

// Each quote is from the linked page, checked on 2026-10-31. Where a provider
// does not say something (for example whether Cloudflare stores visitor IP
// addresses), this page does not say it either.
---
<Base title="Privacy on gethardcount.com" description="What a visit to gethardcount.com sends, and to whom: no cookies, no forms, visits counted with Cloudflare Web Analytics.">
  <section class="top">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p class="lede">This site sets no cookies, and has no forms and no sign-in. This page lists everything a visit sends, and to whom.</p>

      <h2>Visit counts: Cloudflare Web Analytics</h2>
      <p>Each page loads a small script from Cloudflare that reports the visit. Cloudflare says Web Analytics collects “the minimum amount of information - timing metrics”, and its reports show the page, the site you came from, and your country, device type, browser and operating system.</p>
      <p>Cloudflare says it “does not use any client-side state, such as cookies or localStorage”, and “does not track individual end users across our customers’ Internet properties”.</p>
      <p>Like any request, the report reaches Cloudflare with your IP address and your browser’s user agent. Cloudflare says it does not “fingerprint” individuals by these for analytics. Its privacy policy covers what it does with data such as IP addresses.</p>
      <p class="small">
        <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/data-metrics/data-origin-and-collection/">Data origin and collection</a>{' · '}
        <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/">Cloudflare Web Analytics</a>{' · '}
        <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare’s privacy policy</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Hosting: GitHub Pages</h2>
      <p>GitHub Pages serves this site. GitHub says that “when a GitHub Pages site is visited, the visitor’s IP address is logged and stored for security purposes”.</p>
      <p class="small">
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/what-is-github-pages">What is GitHub Pages</a>{' · '}
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub’s privacy statement</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Fonts: Google Fonts</h2>
      <p>The pages load their fonts from Google Fonts, so your browser asks Google’s servers for them, and Google receives your IP address with that request.</p>
      <p class="small"><a href="https://developers.google.com/fonts/faq/privacy">Google Fonts privacy</a></p>

      <h2>Hardcount itself</h2>
      <p>Hardcount, the software, sends no telemetry. When you run it, data goes only to services you set up, such as Stripe and your email server.</p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
      <p class="small muted">Checked on 2026-10-31.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .top { padding-top: 72px; }
  .lede { font-size: 20px; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  h2 { font-size: 26px; margin-top: 1.6em; }
</style>
